        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <h3>How long should an MSc dissertation be?</h3>
        <p>Most MSc dissertations are between 10,000 and 20,000 
        words, but always confirm the exact requirement with your 
        department, as it varies by institution and subject.</p>

        <h3>What makes a good MSc dissertation?</h3>
        <p>A strong dissertation has a clearly defined, appropriately 
        scoped research question; a literature review that critically 
        engages with existing work rather than just summarizing it; a 
        well-justified methodology; and a discussion section that 
        meaningfully connects findings back to the wider research 
        context.</p>

        <h3>Can ChatGPT help with a dissertation?</h3>
        <p>AI tools can be useful for early brainstorming, summarizing 
        sources, or checking grammar, but they can't replace the 
        original research, critical analysis, and academic judgment 
        your dissertation is assessed on. Most universities also have 
        strict policies on AI use in assessed work, so always check 
        your institution's guidelines before relying on it for 
        anything beyond light editing support.</p>

        <h3>How should a master's dissertation be structured?</h3>
        <p>Most MSc dissertations follow: Introduction, Literature 
        Review, Methodology, Findings/Results, Discussion, and 
        Conclusion — though exact chapter names and order can vary 
        by subject and department.</p>

